  "title": "Why the parity comes out even",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "For maps as networks of rule-60 rings with equally spaced law-(12,12) contacts, where a contact persists exactly when every count of closed walks at it in the contact graph is even: (1) over GF(2) a contact c persists exactly when det(I + tN) = det(I + tN without c), N the contact graph mod 2, i.e. when for every m an even number of families of disjoint odd-arc cycles of total length m pass through c; (2) two pairings imply persistence: doubled pairs (both darts of a contact leading to one contact), when every closed walk at c passes one, paired by exchanging the dart at the first such step; and an automorphism of the contact graph fixing c and exchanging its two out-arcs or in-arcs, of which a map flip is a case; (3) FLIP-RULE-20261109-001B's six-contact example persists by two doubled pairs; computed: every persistent contact that sends and hears on a non-degenerate map of up to six contacts has a map flip, a doubled pair or a contact-graph flip, and from seven contacts most have none of them (5,438 of 8,992 at seven, sampled).",
